WEBVTT

00:00.253 --> 00:04.440
Use the page's separate hypothetical change, not a change to the book's database.

00:04.440 --> 00:06.620
A parcel arrives at twelve twenty.

00:06.620 --> 00:10.767
Its original promise was noon; its revised promise is twelve thirty.

00:10.767 --> 00:12.912
The producer wants promised_at to mean the

00:12.912 --> 00:15.731
revised promise, keeping the same name and type.

00:15.731 --> 00:19.280
What changes for an on-time measure and an all-due denominator?

00:19.280 --> 00:20.446
Pause and explain.

00:25.292 --> 00:28.947
The parcel is late against noon, but on time against twelve thirty.

00:28.947 --> 00:32.805
Changing the promise also moves it outside deliveries due by noon.

00:32.805 --> 00:35.846
An unchanged schema does not preserve agreed meaning.

00:35.846 --> 00:39.204
Keep the proposal pending beside the current agreement until reviewed.

00:39.204 --> 00:43.227
The producer and both consuming teams must review their calculations and affected

00:43.227 --> 00:46.249
uses, including consumers missing from recorded dependencies.

00:47.208 --> 00:51.715
The hypothetical alternative keeps promised_at and adds revised_promise_at.

00:51.715 --> 00:56.474
The producer and both consumers approve the design on second November, with effect

00:56.474 --> 01:00.162
from fifth November at one in the afternoon, UTC.

01:00.162 --> 01:04.777
Consumer attachments, validation results and a rollout plan are still required.

01:04.777 --> 01:07.581
Does design approval alone permit publication?

01:07.581 --> 01:10.498
Explain what must happen first, then pause.

01:15.513 --> 01:17.267
Release remains pending.

01:17.267 --> 01:20.982
The steward must confirm the consumer evidence, and the producer must supply

01:20.982 --> 01:22.753
validation and a rollout plan.

01:22.753 --> 01:25.322
Each consumer records its chosen promise rule,

01:25.322 --> 01:27.244
including treatment of an absent revision.

01:27.244 --> 01:29.674
Adding a field does not migrate consumers.

01:29.674 --> 01:35.168
After release, attach actual run, input, output and report refresh references; a

01:35.168 --> 01:36.630
planned date cannot prove delivery.

01:37.552 --> 01:40.289
Keep responsibilities and history explicit.

01:40.289 --> 01:44.722
The steward coordinates review; business teams decide their definitions.

01:44.722 --> 01:48.697
An ownership transfer remains pending until the new team accepts it.

01:48.697 --> 01:53.267
If owners and consumers approve restating history, publish a new output linked to the

01:53.267 --> 01:57.440
old one, the definition, retained inputs, code and approval.

01:57.440 --> 02:00.998
Preserve the earlier report so readers can still explain what they saw.

02:02.068 --> 02:04.150
Try the page's map exercise.

02:04.150 --> 02:10.143
In Data contract's Example record, read deliveries-v1: what does promised_at mean, and

02:10.143 --> 02:12.214
who must agree before it changes?

02:12.214 --> 02:14.586
Explain why keeping the column name and type

02:14.586 --> 02:17.511
is insufficient, then use Check your answer.

02:17.511 --> 02:22.181
The contract records a requirement; it does not establish approval or release.

02:22.181 --> 02:26.375
The page retains the complete alternative field definition and evidence requirements.
